ook Pro we previously tested a  few high-end games on the new 2018 iMac  and we got really good frame rates at  high settings whereas with the MacBook  you'll have trouble unless you hook up  in a GPU now let's get into some video  editing tests which are greatly affected  by graphics performance in our  stabilization tests the iMac finished  couple of seconds faster which can  really add up if you do a lot of  stabilization then exporting a  five-minute 4k h.264 clip there's  actually not much of a difference at all  now exporting an 8-bit h.265 clip the  iMac actually falls behind and takes  over twice as long to export that's  because the new iMac doesn't include  Apple's t2 chip which supports  outstanding hardware HT VC encoding now  exporting a 10-bit h.265 clip the imac  now blows the macbook out of the water  because 10-bit video and final cut  doesn't currently support hardware a TVC  encoding that comes with the t2 chip now  exporting 4.5 K red raw which is very  processor intensive the iMac is quite a  bit faster because the processor and  graphics performance boost over the  MacBook and we see the same thing  exporting 4k 60p Canon C 200 raw the  iMac is now almost twice as fast as the  MacBook and that's not the only  difference what really matters is being  able to edit the 60 frames per second  footage smoothly and that's just what  the iMac does running at a consistent 60  frames per second compared to only 30 on  the MacBook and that's what Vega 20  graphics with the previous high-end 560  X graphics we only got around 17 FPS on  this tests  so overall the iMac outperforms the  MacBook Pro except in the case of 8-bit  HEV see video editing due to the lack of  the t2 chip but for basically everything  else the iMac will perform much better  now let's say you need a Mac for basic  computer tasks or even high-end photo  editing the MacBook should really be  fine and perform close to as well as the  iMac since those tasks don't really use  the graphics 